From d0e445d478833e7681613c684ee5c4feb4f1978c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bulatsaripov <b.sharipov@innopolis.university>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 09:54:17 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] Give the timeline a per-iteration view, and stop calling
 a numeric miss a compile failure

Two defects, found while asking why the profiler arms of the layer study
did no better than the control.

The timeline measured the wrong span. It reports the whole capture --
input generation, JIT compilation, the reference, warmup and every timed
repeat -- so its headline reads "idle holes 4894.123 ms (99.4%); largest
hole 4633.622 ms" while the block being optimized is 0.4% idle and runs
its seven kernels back to back. An author steered by that number goes
hunting starvation that is not there. Reduce to one representative
iteration and give start, end, duration and the idle gap in front of
each launch, with the gap between repeats reported separately: that one
is host dispatch outside the block and fusing cannot remove it, so it
must never be added to the in-block figure. Rows carry the kernel's own
name rather than only the legend alias, because the point of the section
is to be readable without scrolling a 500-row document.

Segmentation anchors on a kernel name, not on a gap threshold -- gap
size is the measurement here, so it cannot also be the premise. The loop
is found as the launch-count group covering the most launches, ignoring
kernels launched once: a real capture has eleven distinct names launched
exactly once, and taking the most common count instead selects 1 and
finds no loop at all.

Numeric misses were reported as compile failures. `metrics` set
compiled, correctness and is_valid from one boolean, so a kernel that
compiled, launched and merely missed tolerance reported compiled=0. The
scheduler reads that as a compile failure and says "rewrite the
candidate", while the branch that says "repair the smallest reported
numerical mismatch" needs compiled and not correctness and so could
never fire for a CuTe task. Across 71 arm worktrees, 44 of 224 failing
turns were numeric near-misses steered into a rewrite; one arm spent all
six turns circling a single rounding-placement error, and a Qwen turn
that missed at 0.0896 against a 0.08 threshold was told to start over.
Replayed against those stored records, every API error still reads as a
compile failure and every near-miss now asks for a repair.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
